WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.800
The walkers on The Walking Dead have&nbsp;
become an iconic group of TV villains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.600
but they didn't get there by doing anything&nbsp;
they want on the screen. Here are the rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.920
walkers have to follow to keep their inherent&nbsp;
walker-ness intact on the hit AMC series.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.320
Shortly after you die in The Walking Dead's&nbsp;
universe, assuming your brains remain intact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.840
you rise as a soulless carcass&nbsp;
and feast upon the warm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.520
wet flesh of the living. But unlike undead&nbsp;
creatures from other, similar media, walkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.400
in The Walking Dead must drag themselves&nbsp;
forward with an exhausted vertical crawl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.080
They can pick up a tiny bit of speed at the sight&nbsp;
of a possible meal, but under no circumstances can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.920
they run or even really jog. If a walker propels&nbsp;
themselves with a velocity roughly equal to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:52.960
zombies pull off in Zombieland, then that walker&nbsp;
is moving way too fast. And hey, that's just fine.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.800
Walkers aren't frightening because of their&nbsp;
athletic prowess. You never have to worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.200
about a single walker chasing you down,&nbsp;
even if that walker won multiple sprint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.080
championships during their pre-walker days.&nbsp;
Most of the time, walkers only become a threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.040
once they've gathered in large enough numbers&nbsp;
to overwhelm a potential victim or victims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.360
or in the occasional instance in which&nbsp;
they successfully sneak up on their prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.520
Both of those things are definitely&nbsp;
frequent enough to make walkers a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.080
major threat, even if they aren't going&nbsp;
to be chasing anyone down anytime soon.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.320
Walkers are not effective or efficient&nbsp;
predators. They're not fast, strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.600
smart, or well-equipped with any offensive&nbsp;
tools except their rotting teeth. They have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.400
no capacity for cunning or any skill with&nbsp;
long-term planning. Most of them aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.280
even aesthetically pleasing, in a manner that&nbsp;
might be useful for luring victims into traps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.040
Their appearance is quite to the contrary, in&nbsp;
fact: Most walkers are so disgusting to behold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.840
they make you want to run screaming&nbsp;
in the opposite direction.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.160
"These things ain't sick! They're&nbsp;
not people! They're dead!"

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.960
Too bad for the walkers, but these circumstances&nbsp;
work out splendidly for those of us here in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.720
real world. Hordes of grotesque and gruesome&nbsp;
undead are visually distinctive in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.600
sticks with the viewer long after the TV has&nbsp;
been shut off. We tune into programs like The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.480
Walking Dead because we want to see the menacing&nbsp;
hordes of the undead, and we want to see them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.120
hideously disfigured. In the world of&nbsp;
The Walking Dead, walkers are terrifying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.640
and in a world where a zombie apocalypse hasn't&nbsp;
happened yet, that's exactly what we want.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.760
Walkers possess several qualities that&nbsp;
inhibit their people-eating abilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.080
while enhancing their entertainment&nbsp;
value. For example, in most cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.760
you can hear them coming from long distances off,&nbsp;
due their apparently compulsive need to make weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.560
barfing noises. Sometimes walkers make&nbsp;
very loud barfing noises, and sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.560
they lower the volume of their barfing noises&nbsp;
to a very quiet level. But one truth remains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:03.840
regardless: Walkers will make uncomfortable,&nbsp;
guttural noises, no matter the situation.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.240
They practically never stop making these&nbsp;
noises, even though they don't seem to serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.600
any purpose for them. In fact,&nbsp;
it gives away their location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.840
and offers potential human meals&nbsp;
plenty of time to run away. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.600
it does add ominous ambiance, and The Walking&nbsp;
Dead wouldn't quite be itself without it.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.880
Walkers can't talk, scream, or sing in&nbsp;
their hideous version of the afterlife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.080
but they don't need to, because those horrible,&nbsp;
hissing moans of theirs haunt our dreams.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:43.600
Look, if you don't absolutely love completely&nbsp;
uncooked, literally fresh-off-the-bone human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.720
meat, then you've got no business being a&nbsp;
walker. It's basically their whole deal.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.240
Walkers lead boring, bland lives, if we're&nbsp;
being honest. They indefinitely roam about with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.440
no purpose or direction, until their limited&nbsp;
abilities of detection pick up on one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.560
dwindling number of humans who have not already&nbsp;
been converted into walkers. They only really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:11.440
want one thing, and it's the thing that keeps the&nbsp;
danger of the show going: The flesh of the living.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.160
The gang of sympathetic survivors on The Walking&nbsp;
Dead often find themselves in life-or-death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.880
conflicts with groups of malignant fellow&nbsp;
humans. While these plots unfold, walkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.400
are regulated to the background, sometimes in a&nbsp;
quite literal sense. Some critics don't like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.480
but unless you just want the walkers&nbsp;
to eat people over and over and over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.520
it's the way it has to be. If the walkers were&nbsp;
the only villains on the show, it would just be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.840
constant buffet of human meat. Feasting on human&nbsp;
flesh is literally the whole gig for a walker.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.640
This is basically the only rule that applies&nbsp;
to both walkers and humans: If your brain dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.200
you die. No exceptions. It doesn't matter&nbsp;
whether you get shot in the face or head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.960
or whether someone pierces your grey&nbsp;
matter with a blade or pointed object.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.520
A swift blow of blunt force could also do the&nbsp;
trick, as long as it punctures the protective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.960
bone layer and squashes the wrinkled ball of goo&nbsp;
within. In any of these cases, that's the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:12.720
the story for you. You can no longer claim the&nbsp;
status of "undead." You're just….well, dead.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.040
The good news for walkers about this rule,&nbsp;
though, is that they can survive virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.480
anything up to this point, including having their&nbsp;
head flat-out removed from their body. Take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.120
walker apart any way you like. As long as they&nbsp;
still have a brain working in their head, they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.920
just watch you do it, which might be all the more&nbsp;
reason to kill the brain and get it over with.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:39.120
It's simple strategy: Any walker hoping to sink&nbsp;
their diseased, blunted teeth into a still-living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.960
screaming person needs to find themselves a&nbsp;
crew. A single walker might get lucky and stumble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.560
upon an injured human or a very stupid child&nbsp;
every now and again, but most of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.120
a walker on their own is basically just a sitting&nbsp;
duck for the next human survivor with a weapon.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:00.160
Sticking with a large group doesn't mean a walker&nbsp;
definitely won't get their brains bashed in at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.200
some point, but hanging with a crowd demonstrably&nbsp;
increases their chances of finding a person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.240
and actually getting a meal out of the deal.&nbsp;
People who see a herd of walkers get scared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:14.240
run away in a random direction, and&nbsp;
sometimes find their way into a dead-end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.320
where walkers can descend upon them. If you're&nbsp;
a walker doing the lone wolf thing, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.480
you're just as likely to end up with a machete&nbsp;
through your head, so stick with your friends.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:29.280
As far as options for the afterlife go, walkerhood&nbsp;
is kind of boring. You stumble around looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.440
fresh meat and, if you find it, attempt to shove&nbsp;
it in your face until there's no more meat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.160
Apart from that, you're just killing time until&nbsp;
someone comes along and caves in your skull.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.600
"You told me you handled&nbsp;
them easily. This is easy?"

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.560
"It's easier than some things."

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:50.000
Being a walker certainly limits your options,&nbsp;
but at least it's easy and low-pressure. Among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.400
walkers, there's no hierarchy. Walkers don't&nbsp;
have walker bosses who tell them what to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.040
Walkers don't get a reward for eating somebody&nbsp;
— they just immediately resume looking for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.960
new person to eat. So whether they eat one person&nbsp;
in a week or 12, it doesn't make any difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:10.640
It doesn't matter if you're a successful walker&nbsp;
or a failure as a walker. Walkers don't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:15.520
compete with each other. In fact, if it wasn't&nbsp;
for the inevitable violent demise, total loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.400
of cognitive function, and endless craving for&nbsp;
flesh, being a walker would be a pretty relaxed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:26.480
gig. They're comfortable living in the moment&nbsp;
— the awful, bloody, post-apocalyptic moment.

